  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT Sun May 10 2026\n\nMonday will be cooler with clearing skies after some morning \ncloud cover. Highs will be mostly in the upper 70s to lower 80s \nwith north to northeast winds 7-14 mph. A surface high pressure \nridge will shift southeast into the area Monday, and the surface \nridge axis will extend from the Ohio Valley southwest into central\nTexas Monday night. Mostly clear skies and radiational cooling \nwill allow temperatures to drop into the lower to mid 50s for \nmorning lows Tuesday.\n\nAn upper level ridge over the southwestern CONUS Monday will \ngradually shift east into Texas Tuesday into Wednesday, and across\nthe state Wednesday into Thursday. With associated subsidence, \nexpect generally clear skies and seasonably very warm \ntemperatures. The warmest temperatures each day will be in the \nnorthern and western Big Country and northwestern Concho Valley. \nForecast highs are expected to range from the mid 80s southeast to\nlower 90s northwest on Tuesday, and from the mid/upper 80s \nsoutheast to mid/upper 90s northwest Wednesday and Thursday. A \ntighter pressure gradient will result in breezy south winds on \nThursday. \n\nBy late week into the weekend, quasi-zonal flow aloft is progged \nover our area. Highs are expected to be a little hotter Friday and\nSaturday with a strengthened low-level thermal ridge edging into \nour area from the west. Also expecting breezy south winds to \ncontinue Friday and Saturday. Will have to watch for the \npossibility Saturday of a weak, embedded disturbance in the flow \naloft to enter the area, a dryline that may approach our western \ncounties in the afternoon, and whether the trailing portion of a \ncold front can move south of the Red River Saturday afternoon. \nWhile this is Day 7 with quite a bit of inherent uncertainty in \nthe details, these features may have the potential to bring a \nchance of showers and thunderstorms to portions of our area.",
